{
  // CI dependency-vulnerability gate (replaces raw `npm audit --audit-level=high`).
  // Fails the build on any high/critical advisory EXCEPT the reviewed, time-boxed
  // exceptions in `allowlist` below. New/unexpected highs still break CI.
  //
  // Run locally with: npm run audit
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  "report-type": "important",
  "allowlist": [
    // Empty on purpose. Two entries have lived here; both were REMOVED by fixing
    // the advisory rather than by re-justifying the exception. Their history is
    // kept, because the STANDING LESSON at the bottom is what both of them cost.

    // GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 (react-router "RSC Mode CSRF Bypass") WAS allowlisted
    // here and was REMOVED 2026-09-14, because the DEFERRAL'S PREMISE EXPIRED.
    //
    // What it claimed, and what was verified and correct when written on
    // 2026-08-04: that the advisory range was `>=7.12.0 <8.3.0`, that "the 7.x
    // line's latest (7.18.2) is still INSIDE that range, so no patch release
    // clears this on 7.x — only the 8.x major does", and that the entry was
    // therefore a deferral with a known cost: an 8.x major migration measured at
    // ~28 files by the 2026-08-03 maintenance sweep.
    //
    // That stopped being accurate on 2026-08-07, when the advisory was updated
    // and stopped carrying ONE range. It carries TWO, non-contiguous:
    //
    //     >= 7.12.0, < 7.18.2   -> first patched 7.18.2
    //     >= 8.0.0,  < 8.3.0    -> first patched 8.3.0
    //
    // The old `<8.3.0` was npm FLATTENING those two into a single span — the
    // identical artifact described in the brace-expansion entry below, hit from
    // the other direction. So the 2026-08-04 comment was not wrong about what it
    // was shown; it was reading a flattened view that hid a published 7.x fix.
    // The exact version it named as vulnerable is the version carrying the fix.
    //
    // CONSEQUENCE FOR THE OPEN 8.x MIGRATION, and the reason this correction is
    // worth its length: the 8.x line is NOT uniformly safe. Migrating onto
    // 8.0-8.2 REINTRODUCES this advisory. The migration must target >= 8.3.0.
    // (With this allowlist empty, CI would now catch that — but after a day of
    // confused work, so it is written down here instead.)
    //
    // The cost of clearing the advisory therefore dropped from a 28-file major
    // migration to a lockfile-only patch bump, and the recorded reason for
    // deferring — "no patch release clears this on 7.x" — became false. Nothing
    // failed when it did. The gate stayed green, because a green gate is
    // precisely what an allowlist entry buys.
    //
    // It did not go unnoticed, which is the part worth keeping: the
    // 2026-08-10 sweep verified the split live against the GitHub advisory API
    // and wrote it up, and BACKLOG.md carried it as an open HIGH from
    // 2026-08-13 naming the exact remedy — "one lockfile bump, one deleted
    // allowlist entry, one test pass". It then sat open for 32 days across four
    // sweeps. The detection worked. The queue is where it died.
    //
    // Fixed properly instead of re-allowlisted: `npm update react-router-dom`
    // moved react-router / react-router-dom 7.18.1 -> 7.18.3 in the lockfile
    // ONLY (`package.json` still declares `^7.18.1`, untouched). After the bump,
    // `npm audit --omit=dev` reports 0 vulnerabilities, `npm run audit` passes
    // with this entry ABSENT, and audit-ci itself reports "Consider not
    // allowlisting advisory: GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2". Verified on the bumped tree:
    // 607 unit tests across 27 files, 64 Playwright e2e tests (route discovery
    // and navigation included), lint unchanged at 0 errors / 12 known warnings.
    //
    // This does NOT make the 8.x migration unnecessary — it removes the security
    // argument for rushing it. That migration keeps its own backlog item.
    //
    // Trigger 2 of the removed entry still stands as a rule for this repo even
    // with the entry gone: if this app stops being a static client-only SPA —
    // i.e. it adds React Router RSC/server mode, ANY route `action`/`loader`
    // that mutates, or an authenticated/mutating server endpoint — re-open this
    // review immediately, because that is the change that would have made the
    // advisory exploitable here.

    // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (brace-expansion DoS) WAS allowlisted here and was
    // REMOVED 2026-08-03, because the entry had quietly stopped being true.
    //
    // What it claimed: that the `overrides` pinned brace-expansion to patched
    // versions (1.1.16 / 5.0.8) and npm audit was raising a FALSE POSITIVE
    // because it flattened the advisory's non-contiguous patched ranges into a
    // single `<=5.0.7` and could not represent the backported 1.x fix.
    //
    // That was accurate when written. It stopped being accurate when the
    // advisory was updated to carry an explicit 1.x range of `<1.1.17`: at that
    // point 1.1.16 was genuinely vulnerable, npm audit was reporting it
    // CORRECTLY, and this entry was suppressing a real high-severity finding
    // rather than a false positive. Nothing failed, which is the problem — the
    // gate stayed green across that change with no signal that the reasoning
    // underneath it had expired.
    //
    // Fixed properly instead of re-allowlisted: the overrides now pin
    // `brace-expansion@1` to ^1.1.18 and `brace-expansion@5` to ^5.0.9, which
    // clears BOTH GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (<1.1.17) and the newer
    // GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 (<1.1.18 on the 1.x line, >=4.0.0 <5.0.9 on 5.x).
    // The 1.x/5.x split is still required — brace-expansion 5.x is ESM-first
    // and crashes eslint's legacy minimatch 3.x — so the two-line override
    // stays; only the versions moved. `npm run audit` now passes with this
    // entry absent, and audit-ci itself reports "Consider not allowlisting
    // advisory: GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg".
    //
    // STANDING LESSON for anything added above: an allowlist entry's
    // justification is only true against the advisory's range AS IT READS TODAY.
    // Ranges get WIDENED (brace-expansion: a version documented as safe became
    // vulnerable) and they get NARROWED (react-router: a fix documented as
    // unreachable became a patch bump). Both directions silently invalidate the
    // reasoning underneath the entry, and NEITHER one fails the gate to tell you.
    // Re-check such an entry against the live range; never trust it because it
    // was reviewed once.
  ]
}
